---
<p class="lede">This chapter covers the pieces every program is made of: named values, numbers, text, decisions, loops, choosing among cases; each section has a program to run.</p>

<p>Chapter 2 used most of these unexplained; here they come one at a time. Some programs are top-level statements, as in <a href="book-01-start.html#scripts">chapter 1</a>; some use <code>main</code>. A file has one entry point, so it uses one style.</p>

<h2 id="bindings">Naming values: <code>let</code>, <code>var</code>, <code>const</code></h2>
<example name="book-03-bindings"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>let</code> names a value that does not change; start with it.</li>
  <li><code>var</code> names a value you can change later: <code>visits += 1</code> adds one.</li>
  <li><code>const</code> names a value known at compile time, for fixed values like limits and sizes.</li>
  <li>The type is optional on <code>let</code> and <code>var</code>; the compiler infers it: <code>let age: i64 = 36</code> asks for <code>i64</code>, <code>let n = 3</code> is an <code>i32</code>, the whole-number default.</li>
  <li>Underscores in a number are for you: <code>86_400</code> is 86400.</li>
</ul>
<p>Change a <code>let</code> and the compiler stops you and shows the fix:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `visits` is immutable
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> immutable.byn:2:1

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> visits += 1
<span class="gut">    │</span> ^^^^^^ declared with `let` on line 1
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: declare it with `var` if it should change</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 1 │</span> var visits = 0
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add"> +++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2104</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check immutable.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>immutable.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The marker points at the use, the label names the <code>let</code>, the help shows the edit.</p>
<p>Prefer <code>let</code>: a <code>var</code> says the value changes; look for where.</p>

<h3 id="shadowing">Reusing a name</h3>
<p>Bayan does not let an inner scope hide an outer name; it is an error:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2007:</span> `input` is already a local here
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> shadow.byn:3:9

<span class="gut">  3 │</span>     let input = "inner"
<span class="gut">    │</span>         ^^^^^ already declared on line 1
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: pick a different name; Bayan does not let a local hide another one</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2007</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check shadow.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>shadow.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The same scope can redeclare the name: a fresh binding, with a different type:</p>
<example name="book-03-shadow"/>
<p><code>input</code> was a string; after the second <code>let</code> it is 5, so <code>input + 1</code> is 6. The old string is no longer reachable under that name.</p>

<h2 id="numbers">Numbers</h2>
<p>Bayan has fixed-size integers and two floating-point types: signed <code>i8</code>…<code>i64</code>, unsigned <code>u8</code>…<code>u64</code>, and <code>f32</code>/<code>f64</code>; a literal like <code>1.5</code> is an <code>f64</code>.</p>
<example name="book-03-numbers"/>
<table>
  <tr><th>Output</th><th>What it shows</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>22 12 85 3 2</code></td><td>Integer division rounds toward zero: <code>17 / 5</code> is 3, <code>17 % 5</code> is 2; a negative dividend gives a negative remainder (<code>-7 % 3</code> is <code>-1</code>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>3.4</code></td><td>Exact with <code>f64</code> operands.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>350</code></td><td><code>i64(small)</code> converts, the type written like a function; widening <code>u8</code> to <code>i64</code> cannot lose anything.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>3000000000 true</code></td><td>Above 2,147,483,647 does not fit <code>i32</code>, so it needs <code>i64</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>255 10 1000</code></td><td>Hex (<code>0xFF</code>), binary (<code>0b1010</code>) and exponent (<code>1e3</code>) literals.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>8.5 7</code></td><td><code>f64</code> to integer drops the fraction (<code>i64(7.9)</code> is 7); it does not round.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>300 0</code></td><td><code>T.tryFrom(x)</code> gives <code>null</code> when the value does not fit: 300 fits <code>i64</code>, not <code>u8</code>, so <code>??</code> supplies 0. <a href="book-06-unions.html#optionals">Chapter 6</a> explains <code>?</code> types.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3 id="numeric-errors">When a number does not fit or does not match</h3>
<p>A literal too big for its type is a compile error:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2203:</span> 300 does not fit in `u8`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> range.byn:1:17

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> let small: u8 = 300
<span class="gut">    │</span>                 ^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: use a value from 0 to 255, the range of `u8`</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2203</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check range.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>range.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Different number types never mix silently: an <code>i64</code> times an <code>f64</code> is an error:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2208:</span> `*` cannot be applied to `i64` and `f64`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> mixed.byn:3:10

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> print($"{count * ratio}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: convert one side explicitly, e.g. `(f64)…`; mixed signedness never converts implicitly</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2208</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check mixed.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>mixed.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The message says <code>*</code> cannot be applied to the two types; the help says to convert one side by type name, <code>f64(count) * ratio</code>, not the <code>(f64)…</code> cast it shows. The same holds for a rune and an integer (<code>let n: i32 = 'x'</code> is refused; use <code>i32(c)</code>) and for <code>f64</code> to <code>f32</code> (<code>f32(r)</code>).</p>

<h2 id="strings">Strings</h2>
<p>A <code>string</code> is UTF-8: <code>length</code> counts bytes, <code>runeCount</code> counts characters (Unicode code points, called runes).</p>
<example name="book-03-strings"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>"salām"</code> is 6 bytes and 5 runes: <code>ā</code> takes two bytes.</li>
  <li><code>word[0..1]</code> slices by byte offsets: <code>0..1</code> is the first byte, <code>s</code>. Slicing mid-rune is not what you want.</li>
  <li><code>word.runes</code> walks the runes; <code>for ch in "héy"</code> does the same.</li>
  <li><code>"""…"""</code> is a multi-line string.</li>
  <li><code>$"…{expr}…"</code> interpolates; any expression goes in the braces. Double a literal brace: <code>{{</code> and <code>}}</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Strings have no <code>+</code>; interpolation replaces it:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2209:</span> strings cannot be joined with `+`; use interpolation
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> stringplus.byn:3:7

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> print(a + b)
<span class="gut">    │</span>       ^^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: write the parts as one interpolation: `$"{a}{b}"`, or `buf.append($"{a}{b}")` in a loop</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 3 │</span> print($"{a}{b}")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">       +++++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2209</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check stringplus.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>stringplus.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The compiler writes the correct line. A character (<code>'x'</code>) uses single quotes and has type <code>rune</code>.</p>
<p>Indexing a string with one number is refused too: a position in UTF-8 is not a character:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2201:</span> a `string` cannot be indexed
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> stringindex.byn:2:11

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> print(word[0])
<span class="gut">    │</span>           ^^^ strings have no indexer
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: read a byte, slice, or take characters; `string` is UTF-8, so position 0 is not a character:</span>
          `word.bytes[0]`       the byte at offset 0 (applied below)
          `word[a..b]`          the text between byte offsets a and b
          `word.runes.take(n)`  the first n runes
          `word.graphemes`      user-perceived characters
<span class="gut">    │ 2 │</span> print(word.bytes[0])
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">           ++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2201</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check stringindex.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>stringindex.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The help lists the four things you probably meant; a diagnostic's first line says what is wrong, its help names the choices.</p>

<h2 id="if">Deciding: <code>if</code>, <code>unless</code> and the ternary</h2>
<example name="book-03-if"/>
<ul>
  <li>The condition has no parentheses and the body always has braces.</li>
  <li><code>else if</code> chains; <code>else</code> must start on the closing brace's line.</li>
  <li><code>cond ? a : b</code> picks one of two values.</li>
  <li><code>unless cond { … }</code> runs when the condition is false.</li>
  <li>A statement can end with <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code>: <code>print("take an umbrella") if raining</code>. The condition is checked first; use it for guards such as <code>return if n &lt; 0</code>.</li>
  <li><code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code> and <code>!</code> are and, or, not; they take <code>bool</code> operands.</li>
</ul>
<p>The condition must be a <code>bool</code>, not a number:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `bool`, found `i32`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> cond.byn:2:4

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> if n {
<span class="gut">    │</span>    ^

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2202</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check cond.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>cond.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>An <code>else</code> on its own line is refused; the help shows where to move it:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1041:</span> `else` must follow `}` on the same line
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> elseline.byn:5:1

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> else {
<span class="gut">    │</span> ^^^^ on a new line
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: move `else` up to the line of `}`</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 4 │</span> } else {
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">  +</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN1041</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check elseline.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>elseline.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A comparison with <code>null</code> uses <code>is null</code>:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2027:</span> compare with `is null` / `is not null`, not `==`
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> nullcmp.byn:2:4

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> if name == null {
<span class="gut">    │</span>    ^^^^^^^^^^^^
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: write `name is null`</span>
<span class="gut">    │ 2 │</span> if name is null {
<span class="gut">    │   │</span><span class="add">         ++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN2027</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check nullcmp.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>nullcmp.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="loops">Repeating: <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>do</code></h2>
<example name="book-03-loops"/>
<ul>
  <li><code>0..3</code> runs from 0 up to but not including 3; <code>1..=3</code> includes the end.</li>
  <li><code>for n in [4, 8, 15]</code> walks a list literal; the same form walks any list, map or string's runes.</li>
  <li><code>while</code> tests first; <code>do { … } while cond</code> runs the body once before testing.</li>
  <li><code>continue</code> skips to the next pass and <code>break</code> leaves the loop; both accept a trailing <code>if</code>.</li>
  <li>A label names a loop so an inner loop can act on an outer one: <code>outer: for …</code>, then <code>continue outer</code> or <code>break outer</code>. The output shows it: when <code>col</code> passes <code>row</code> the inner loop is abandoned; at <code>row == 2</code> both end.</li>
</ul>
<p>A statement whose value is discarded does nothing; the compiler says so:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1012:</span> this expression does nothing as a statement
  <span class="gut">--&gt;</span> unused.byn:2:1

<span class="gut">  2 │</span> total + 1
<span class="gut">    │</span> ^^^^^^^^^ the value is computed and discarded
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help: write `_ = f()` to discard a call's result; only calls, assignments and `++`/`--` can stand</span>
        alone

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN1012</span></pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler (<code>bayan check unused.byn</code>). The path is shortened to <code>unused.byn</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Only calls, assignments and <code>++</code>/<code>--</code> can stand alone. The help suggests <code>_ = f()</code> to discard a result; that form does not work yet, so write <code>let _ = f()</code>.</p>

<h2 id="switch">Choosing among many: <code>switch</code></h2>
<example name="book-03-switch"/>
<ul>
  <li>The <strong>expression</strong> form, <code>value switch { pattern =&gt; result, … }</code>, produces a value. Patterns are a literal (<code>0</code>), a comparison (<code>&lt; 0</code>), alternatives (<code>1 or 2</code>), a range of conditions (<code>&gt;= 3 and &lt; 10</code>), or <code>_</code> for everything else; the first match wins. The arms must cover every value, hence the last <code>_</code>; without it the compiler says <code>switch</code> does not handle every <code>i32</code> (<code>BYN2410</code>).</li>
  <li>The <strong>statement</strong> form, <code>switch value { case … : … default: … }</code>, runs code. <code>case "stop" or "halt":</code> matches either; <code>case let w when w.length &gt; 3:</code> binds the value and adds a condition.</li>
</ul>
<p>Chapter 6 uses the same <code>switch</code> to take apart unions and enums, where leaving out a case is a compile error.</p>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<example name="book-03-q1" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>3 2 3.4</code></pre>
  <p>Integer <code>17 / 5</code> is 3 and <code>17 % 5</code> is 2; with both as <code>f64</code> the division is exact, 3.4. <code>3.4 2</code> assumes division keeps the fraction: the operand type decides, not the context.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-03-q2" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>8</code></pre>
  <p>The loop runs <code>i</code> = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5; at 2 <code>continue</code> skips the addition, at 5 <code>break</code> ends the loop before it, so <code>n</code> is 0 + 1 + 3 + 4. Wrong answers: 13 adds 5, but <code>break if i == 5</code> comes before the <code>+=</code>; 10 forgets that <code>0..6</code> starts at 0 and ends before 6.</p>
</details>
<example name="book-03-q3" output="no"/>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>What does this print?</summary>
  <p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>medium large small</code></pre>
  <p>3 matches <code>&gt;= 3 and &lt; 10</code> (<code>medium</code>); 10 fails that range and reaches <code>_</code> (<code>large</code>); 2 matches <code>1 or 2</code> (<code>small</code>). The first match wins, so order matters when patterns overlap.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>You write <code>let total = 0</code>, then <code>total += price</code> in a loop. What happens, and what is the fix?</summary>
  <p><code>BYN2104</code>: <code>total</code> is immutable, declared with <code>let</code>, and the help suggests <code>var total = 0</code>. The program does not run: the problem is the declaration, so changing the loop does not help.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz">
  <summary>Which of these lines compiles? (a) <code>let s = "a" + "b"</code> (b) <code>let s = $"{"a"}{"b"}"</code> (c) <code>if count { … }</code> where <code>count</code> is an <code>i32</code></summary>
  <p>Only (b). (a): strings have no <code>+</code>, and <code>BYN2209</code> says to interpolate. (c): an <code>if</code> condition must be <code>bool</code> (<code>BYN2202</code>); write <code>count != 0</code>.</p>
</details>

